(Generative AI in Undergraduate Education: Impacts on Critical Thinking) Tj T*
(Authors: Smith, J. R., & Lee, H. J. \(2023\)) Tj T*
(Journal: Studies in Higher Education, 48\(11\), 1742-1758.) Tj T*
(DOI: https://doi.org/10.1080/03075079.2023.2189401) Tj T*
(---------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj T*
(ABSTRACT:) Tj T*
(This empirical study examines the relationship between generative AI reliance) Tj T*
(and critical thinking disposition among university students over one semester.) Tj T*
(Sample size: N = 315 undergraduate students assessed via Cornell Critical Thinking Test.) Tj T*
(---------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj T*
(KEY EMPIRICAL FINDINGS:) Tj T*
(1. Passive Dependency Risk: Students who copied AI responses without critical review) Tj T*
(   showed a 22.4% decline in analytical reasoning and argument evaluation scores \(p = 0.002\).) Tj T*
(2. Active Verification Benefit: Students who cross-checked AI outputs against primary) Tj T*
(   academic sources improved critical evaluation scores by 14.2%.) Tj T*
(3. Threshold Effect: Over-reliance \(>12 hours/week of unguided AI usage\) correlated) Tj T*
(   negatively with independent problem-solving retention \(r = -0.31\).) Tj T*
ET
